<p>End-of-service gratuity is one of the more confusing parts of UAE compensation — it's not paid monthly, it's not guaranteed to be a specific amount until you actually leave, and it's calculated on your <em>basic</em> salary, not your total package. Here's how to think about it.</p>

<h2>The General Principle</h2>
<p>UAE labor law entitles most private-sector employees who complete at least one year of continuous service to a gratuity payment upon leaving, calculated from their basic salary — not including housing, transport, or other allowances. The exact formula and entitlement can vary based on your contract type and length of service, so always confirm the specifics with your employer's HR team or the official UAE government resources rather than relying on a generic estimate.</p>

<h2>Why "Basic Salary" Matters So Much</h2>
<p>Because gratuity is calculated on basic salary rather than total compensation, how your package is split between "basic" and "allowances" during salary negotiation directly affects your eventual gratuity payout — a package heavy on allowances and light on basic salary will generally produce a smaller gratuity than an equivalent total package weighted the other way. This is one of the negotiation points worth understanding before you sign, alongside the housing and insurance factors covered in our <a href="/blog/salary-negotiation-guide-dubai">Dubai salary negotiation guide</a>.</p>

<h2>Don't Treat It as Your Emergency Fund</h2>
<p>It's tempting to mentally count gratuity as part of your safety net, but there are two practical problems: it's only paid when you actually leave (voluntarily or otherwise), and disputes or delays around final settlements do happen. Treat gratuity as a bonus that shortens your runway if you leave — not as a substitute for liquid emergency savings you can access immediately. See our full breakdown in <a href="/blog/emergency-fund-calculator-uae">how much your UAE emergency fund should be</a>.</p>

<h2>Plan Around It, Don't Rely on It</h2>
<p>A few practical habits:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Get your gratuity terms in writing</strong> as part of your offer or contract, so there's no ambiguity later.</li>
  <li><strong>Track your basic salary separately</strong> from your total package so you always know roughly what your gratuity would be if you left today.</li>
  <li><strong>Don't spend against an expected gratuity payout</strong> — treat it as a bonus that arrives later, not income you can count on for near-term plans.</li>
  <li><strong>Revisit the numbers after every raise or role change</strong>, since a change in basic salary changes your gratuity calculation going forward.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Keep the Rest of Your Finances Visible</h2>
<p>Gratuity is one piece of a much larger financial picture — the more useful daily habit is knowing your real Safe-to-Spend number and savings rate right now, not just your eventual payout. <a href="/">FinTrack</a> reads your UAE bank statement and shows exactly where you stand today, so gratuity can stay what it should be: a long-term bonus, not a load-bearing part of your monthly plan.</p>

